.page gapsh 1 the Gensokyo shell

# SYNOPSIS
*gapsh* [*-li*] [*-c* _COMMAND_ [_NAME_ [_ARGUMENT_...]]]
*gapsh* [_SCRIPT_ [_ARGUMENT_...]]

# DESCRIPTION
`gapsh` is the command interpreter. Every command passes through a gap in the Boundary, which is why it is called that. It reads commands from the terminal, from a script file or from the `-c` option, and runs them. The language is a practical subset of the Bourne shell family; see gapsh-language(7). The builtin commands are listed in gapsh-builtins(7).

Programs are looked up in the directories of `PATH`, which the login profile sets to `/bin:/sbin:/usr/bin`. A command that cannot be found is reported with the name of the closest program in `/bin`, if there is one: `Did Yukari's gap lead you astray? Perhaps you meant 'ls'.`

# OPTIONS
@ *-c* _COMMAND_
    Run _COMMAND_ and exit. Extra operands set `$0` and the positional parameters.
@ *-l*
    Act as a login shell: read `/Hakurei/etc/profile` and show `/Hakurei/etc/motd` on starting.
@ *-i*
    Behave as an interactive shell even without a terminal on the error output.
@ *-s*, *-e*, *-x*
    Accepted and ignored.

Without `-c` and without a script the shell reads standard input; it is interactive if that is a terminal.

# STARTUP
A login shell reads `/Hakurei/etc/profile`. The interactive and the login shell then read `~/.gaprc` if it exists. A script is run without either. The `/etc/profile` of this system sets `PATH`, `TERM`, `EDITOR` (pnote) and `PAGER` (patchouli-pager) and defines the aliases `ll`, `la`, `l`, `..`, `grep`, `cls`, `shutdown`, `exorcise`, `spellcards` and `netherworld`; see profile(5).

# THE PROMPT
The default prompt is `reimu@gensokyo ~ $`, the user in shrine red, the host in white, the directory in cyan, with the previous exit status in front when it was not zero. The sigil is `#` for root. Set `PS1` to change it; these escapes are understood in its value: `\u` user, `\h` host, `\w` directory (with `~` for home), `\W` its last component, `\$` the sigil, `\n` a newline, `\e` the escape character, `\\` a backslash. A continuation line (after an unfinished quote or `if`) is prompted with `>`.

# LINE EDITING
@ Enter
    Run the line.
@ Left, Right, ^B, ^F; Home, ^A; End, ^E
    Move the cursor. Alt-B and Alt-F move by words.
@ Backspace, Delete, ^D
    Delete before / under the cursor. ^D on an empty line leaves the shell.
@ ^K, ^U, ^W, Alt-D
    Cut to the end of the line, to the start, the word before the cursor, the word after it.
@ ^Y
    Paste what was cut last.
@ ^T
    Swap the two characters before the cursor.
@ Up, Down
    Walk through the history.
@ ^R
    Search the history backwards as you type; Enter accepts, Esc cancels.
@ Tab
    Complete a command name (first word) or a file name. A second Tab lists the possibilities.
@ ^L
    Clear the screen.
@ ^C
    Abandon the line being typed.

The history keeps the last 1000 lines in memory and saves the last 500 in `~/.gapsh_history`. The builtin `history` lists it.

# JOBS
A command ending in `&` runs in the background; the shell prints its job number and process id. Finished background jobs are reported before the next prompt. `jobs`, `fg` and `bg` manage them. The shell itself ignores ^C, ^\ and ^Z; programs started from it get the default behaviour, so ^C interrupts the foreground job.

# EXIT STATUS
The status of the last command; 127 if a script cannot be read; 2 for a bad option or a syntax error in `-c`.

# FILES
@ /Hakurei/etc/profile
    Read by login shells.
@ /Hakurei/etc/motd
    Shown by login shells. A `\e` in it is the escape character, so it can carry colours.
@ ~/.gaprc
    Read by interactive shells.
@ ~/.gapsh_history
    The saved history.

# EXAMPLES
    gapsh -c 'echo $((6*7))'
    gapsh script.sh one two

# SEE ALSO
gapsh-language(7), gapsh-builtins(7), profile(5), keys(7), kappabox(1)
